  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: 'The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the correction.' Reply A is: ```json5\\n{\"a\": 1}\\n```\\n{\"b\": 2}. Reply B is: {\"b\": 2}. The rule requires Reply A to yield {\"a\": 1}, because the json5 fence is a code fence that holds an object, and it is the only fenced object, so the last one inside a code fence is {\"a\": 1}. The rule requires Reply B to yield {\"b\": 2}, because there is no code fence, so the last top-level object is {\"b\": 2}. The check, however, returns {\"b\": 2} for both replies: its fence regex only recognizes json, JSON, or untagged fences, so it ignores the json5 fence, and its top-level scan then finds {\"b\": 2} as the last top-level object in both. This conjecture would be shown false if recover_json_object returned {\"a\": 1} for Reply A and {\"b\": 2} for Reply B, or otherwise gave different answers for the two replies."
